.\" nm.1 - list symbols from object files
.Dd April 19, 2026
.Dt NM 1
.Os Substrate
.Sh NAME
.Nm nm
.Nd list symbols from object files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl aABCDglnoPprSsuVv
.Op Fl t Ar radix
.Op Fl -format Ns = Ns Ar format
.Op Fl -radix Ns = Ns Ar radix
.Op Fl -demangle
.Op Fl -defined-only
.Op Fl -no-weak
.Op Fl -extern-only
.Op Fl -undefined-only
.Op Fl -debug-syms
.Op Fl -numeric-sort
.Op Fl -no-sort
.Op Fl -reverse-sort
.Op Fl -size-sort
.Op Fl -print-file-name
.Op Fl -print-size
.Op Fl -dynamic
.Op Fl -print-armap
.Op Fl -special-syms
.Op Ar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility displays the symbol table of each ELF object file named on the
command line.
If no file is given,
.Pa a.out
is used.
.Pp
For each symbol, the output includes its value (address), type letter,
and name.
The exact format depends on the selected output mode.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl a , Fl -debug-syms
Display all symbols, including debugger-only symbols that are not
normally listed (e.g.,
.Dv STT_FILE
and
.Dv STT_SECTION
symbols).
.It Fl A , Fl o , Fl -print-file-name
Prepend the file name to each output line.
.It Fl B
Use BSD output format (default).
.It Fl C , Fl -demangle
Demangle C++, Rust, and D symbol names into their original form.
.It Fl D , Fl -dynamic
Display the dynamic symbol table
.Pq Dv .dynsym
instead of the normal symbol table
.Pq Dv .symtab .
.It Fl g , Fl -extern-only
Display only external (global and weak) symbols.
.It Fl l
Display source file name and line number from DWARF debugging
information (not yet implemented).
.It Fl n , Fl v , Fl -numeric-sort
Sort symbols numerically by their value (address) instead of
alphabetically by name.
.It Fl p , Fl -no-sort
Do not sort; display symbols in symbol table order.
.It Fl P , Fl -portability
Use POSIX.2 output format:
.Dq name type value size .
.It Fl r , Fl -reverse-sort
Reverse the sort order.
.It Fl S , Fl -print-size
Print the size of defined symbols in BSD format.
.It Fl s , Fl -print-armap
Print the archive index (symbol map) when processing archives.
.It Fl t Ar radix
Use
.Ar radix
for printing symbol values.
.Ar radix
may be
.Sq d
for decimal,
.Sq o
for octal, or
.Sq x
for hexadecimal (default).
.It Fl u , Fl -undefined-only
Display only undefined symbols.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print version information and exit.
.It Fl -format Ns = Ns Ar fmt
Select output format:
.Sq bsd
(default),
.Sq posix ,
or
.Sq sysv .
.It Fl -radix Ns = Ns Ar r
Equivalent to
.Fl t ;
.Ar r
may be
.Sq d ,
.Sq o ,
or
.Sq x .
.It Fl -defined-only
Display only defined symbols.
.It Fl -no-weak
Exclude weak symbols from the output.
.It Fl -size-sort
Sort symbols by size (largest first), with ties broken by name.
.It Fl -special-syms
Include target-specific special symbols in the output.
.El
.Sh "SYMBOL TYPE LETTERS"
Each symbol is classified with a single letter indicating its type.
Uppercase letters denote global (external) symbols; lowercase letters
denote local symbols.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "X" -compact
.It T
Text (code) section symbol.
.It D
Initialized data section symbol.
.It B
BSS (uninitialized data) section symbol.
.It R
Read-only data section symbol.
.It A
Absolute symbol (not associated with a section).
.It C
Common symbol.
.It U
Undefined symbol.
.It W
Weak symbol (not specifically tagged as a data object).
.It V
Weak object symbol.
.It N
Debug symbol.
.It n
Read-only data in a non-allocated section.
.It ?
Unknown symbol type.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success, or 1 if any input file could not be processed or
recognized.
.Sh EXAMPLES
List symbols from an object file:
.Dl $ nm main.o
.Pp
List only undefined symbols:
.Dl $ nm -u libfoo.a
.Pp
List symbols sorted by address with demangling:
.Dl $ nm -Cn myprogram
.Pp
Use POSIX output format with decimal values:
.Dl $ nm -P -td myprogram
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ar 1 ,
.Xr ld 1 ,
.Xr objdump 1 ,
.Xr readelf 1 ,
.Xr size 1 ,
.Xr strip 1 ,
.Xr elf 5
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is broadly compatible with the POSIX.1-2008 specification and
GNU
.Nm .
.Sh AUTHORS
Substrate contributors.
